WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.340
- Okay, very excited to be here today

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.290
to talk to you about work that
I've been doing as a postdoc

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.180
on the evolution of plant
shapes using these models

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.100
that we've been hearing
a lot about this morning.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.940
So I think there was a good primer for the

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.640
work that I'm gonna present here

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.270
on many of the talks that we saw today.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.430
I'm gonna focus on the
biological questions

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.979
and how those models can be applied

00:00:32.979 --> 00:00:35.773
to a very specific biological question.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.212
I wanna start by just showing
you this very basic pattern

00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:44.212
of biodiversity, the distribution
of organisms on Earth

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.400
varies according to where you
are and what location you are.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.250
This is a map showing the
number of vertebrate species.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.370
So the hot colors are the locations

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.070
that contain many species.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.130
And you can see that
it varies by latitude,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.110
meaning the distance north
or south of the Equator.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.450
So we see there are many
more species in the tropics

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.150
than there are contained in
the temperate environments

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.230
in North America and Asia.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.560
One explanation for those differences

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.140
is that there are stark
climatic differences

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.870
between locations such that some locations

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.220
can afford the many
higher numbers of species

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.000
than are located in others, so this would

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.270
just be a comparison to say,

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.920
the Saharan Desert and
the tropics of Peru.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.850
But there are also locations
where it seems to be

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.430
much more climatic similarity.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.270
This looks like a very lush
forest in British Columbia,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.340
but there's still many fewer species

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.143
contained in this forest
than there are in Peru.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.570
This is a pattern that
we see across all taxa,

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.430
this is not just specific plants or birds,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.020
it's across many varied groups of animals

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:55.220
and in particular, it is true that plants

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.650
generally follow a latitudinal
diversity gradient.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.460
So here I'm just showing you the number

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.680
of flowering tree species on the X-axis.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.070
And that number kinda binned according

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.340
to where you are in the
Earth and you can see

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.040
on this plot, that there
are many more species

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.570
contained in the equatorial latitudes

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.020
than there are outside of it.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.510
But one component, so we
have known this pattern

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.500
for centuries essentially,
and biologists have

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.840
really been vexed by it
and we want to understand

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.190
why it exists and how it is related

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:30.360
to other aspects of biodiversity
that we're interested in.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.590
So not all species are the same as saying

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.140
there are this many number of ecological

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.060
groupings in those locations.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.070
So we wanna kind of tease
apart the number of species

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.910
from the number of ecologies.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.620
One example of the way
that not all species

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.650
are ecologically unique
is an example from birds.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.450
The Zosterops genus of birds
is a group of white-eyes.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.750
So this is the white-eye
here down this image

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.450
and the image that I'm showing you

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.560
over there, is six different
species of white-eyes.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.280
And so to the lay observer,
it might just look

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.830
all like the same bird, right?

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.600
So, what's true is that,
just because there are

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.020
six different species,
really we could call these

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.030
kind of the same ecology.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.890
There are six species, but
maybe one ecological function.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:28.840
And so we wanna tease apart
these aspects of say, shape

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.400
from the other taxonomic diversity

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.320
that we described in
museums for centuries.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.260
So we know that species that are similar

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.330
in shape and size perform a
similar ecological function.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.270
Which raises two questions.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.430
The first is how does shape diversity

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.730
vary according to latitude?

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.510
We know that the number of species

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.210
changes according to latitude.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:56.120
And that informs another and
deeper fundamental question

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.999
about evolution biology which is,

00:03:57.999 --> 00:04:01.300
whether species at different latitudes

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:04.610
have some sort of different
ability to carve up

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.920
the ecological space in those places.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.099
So is it that tropical
species are really just able

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:14.240
to become more finely
and specifically adapted

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.140
towards one ecological
function, and that's the reason

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.000
why we've jammed so many more species

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.253
in those locations than otherwise?

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.740
So plant leaf shapes are obviously
very diverse and complex.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.150
We've certainly all
walked around in a forest

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.740
and looked around and seen many
different shapes and sizes.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.150
There's just a schematic
there of some of the different

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:41.000
shapes of leaves as they're,
in particular species,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.650
as they develop, so there's
obviously also changes

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.110
according to development.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.100
But there's also this very basic pattern

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.280
that's known in plant shape
according to latitude,

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.890
which is that high latitude temperate

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:57.120
locations have more toothed leaves.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.800
So the edges of this
leaf are called toothed

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.630
and this tropical leaf has
what's called an entire margin

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.130
and so the absence of tooths
in tropical locations.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.000
So evolutionary biologists
have been trying

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.900
to study this difference
and we're kind of interested

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.970
in a next level of complexity.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:15.660
One that doesn't necessarily
involve just saying

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.790
this is toothed, this is not,
but something more complex.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:23.638
So we've heard a lot this
morning about the magic

00:05:23.638 --> 00:05:27.050
of computer vision and
how it might help us

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.490
and reduce some of this complexity

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.350
to understand shape differences.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.390
So we focus our study
on ferns of the world.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.200
There are about 12,000 species

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.973
of ferns distributed globally.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.080
Here are two such examples.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.960
Two specimens that are contained

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.920
in the US National Herbarium

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.950
and they're two different species.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:51.960
I think it's fairly obvious
that they look very different

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.860
and that difference is very complex.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.990
So it might be difficult to measure

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.500
the difference between those two organisms

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.930
except for if we might use something like

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:07.143
a convolutional neural net
to extract that information.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.460
The distribution of ferns of the world

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.180
follows these kind of similar pattern

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:17.490
to what we find among other organisms.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.280
There are many more species
contained in the tropics.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.230
This is actually just a quick estimate

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.090
of the number of species
that might be located

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:31.010
in different places on
Earth, using a dataset

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.100
that we heard about
this morning called GBIF

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.500
or the Global Biodiversity
Information Facility.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.490
So I took all of the occurrences

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.670
that existed on GBIF for
ferns and mapped them

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.300
according to where they were found.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.409
These are for all occurrences that had

00:06:46.409 --> 00:06:49.170
some decimal lat and long.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.750
We find that again, there
are many more species

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:53.340
contained in the tropics.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.490
Ferns are also highly diverse
in both size and shape.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.267
So, here are eight specimens
of eight different species.

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:05.200
You can see that there's
really a lot of complexity here

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.380
that we'd like to understand,
but it's not so obvious

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:12.070
how we might get to that answer

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.340
if not for some large scale method

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.760
that would help us put
all these eight species

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.900
in some sort of high dimensional,
or low dimensional space

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.500
based on their differences which are

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.000
clearly very high dimensional.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.640
So ferns have been completely digitized

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:29.670
at the US National Herbarium.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.249
We heard about the digitization
effort this morning.

00:07:33.249 --> 00:07:36.370
I just wanna show you, this
is kind of what an herbarium

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:40.170
looks like, it looks almost
nothing like that normally.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.610
That is just when you wanna
get a really cool image

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.870
for everyone to look at,
normally it's just a bunch

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.050
of file cabinets that are closed.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.890
But there are flat specimens
that we can then digitize

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:53.380
which is a really useful way in which

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.973
we've kind of moved
forward with this project.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.280
So our approach was to
take all of the specimens

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.040
that are contained, all
the digitized specimens

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:08.440
that we could find for ferns,
not only in the Smithsonian,

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:13.440
but also on a larger, kind of
international collaboration,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:15.450
mostly focused on North America called

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.730
The Integrated Digitized
Biocollections or iDigBio.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:20.790
So we took images from iDigBio as well

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:25.120
as images from Smithsonian
to train three models.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.670
The first model was to
identify, or label the specimen

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.280
according to the genus of the plant,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:35.020
of the fern on the image.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:38.360
And we only, we built kind of
this as our simplest model,

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.790
which is that we took genera that had

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:45.100
at least 500 occurrences in our dataset.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.540
So we've kind of eluded to it all day,

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.360
but it's more difficult to learn features

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.010
when you have fewer training examples.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:55.400
In this case, we wanted there to be

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.660
at least 500 training
examples for each genus.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:01.540
But we started to change
that up as we found

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.590
that our original model
we incredibly accurate,

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.180
so we wanted to kinda
test the limits of it.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.710
We made a model where there
were 50 specimens per genus.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:12.700
So a lot of this is
related to where botanists

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.690
have collected plants around the world

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:17.240
and how those collections have been built.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.830
So there's somewhat of
that biased in terms of,

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.295
you know, where the
specimens are distributed

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:24.520
and which specimens we found.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:28.560
But partly, rare specimens
tend to come from places

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.060
that botanists don't visit very often

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.903
and might be unique, so
that's kind of why we

00:09:31.903 --> 00:09:33.760
kind of moved around like this.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.840
So we built a genera model with 269 genera

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.590
for about 50 specimens and then,

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:42.700
ultimately, a 1,425 species model

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:46.440
with at least 50 specimens per species.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.270
So in each case, the
model gets more complex

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.050
and it will be more
challenging for our model

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.790
to distinguish between
the different labels

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.803
that we're going to give it.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.850
So we talked a little bit this morning

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:06.240
about confusion matrices,
but this is essentially

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.590
how we validate the model
after we've trained it.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:12.840
In the first case we had
hundreds of thousands of images

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.870
and we carved up our training set into 80%

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.400
of those images that we
would train our data with,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.800
and 20% of those images,
which it had never seen,

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.690
we're now going to ask
how good is it at giving

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.540
the correct label that
we actually already know.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.700
So for a given genus, the actual genus A,

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:34.012
we have some proportion of the labels

00:10:34.012 --> 00:10:39.012
that the neural net then
generates for that genus.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:42.650
And we can plot those
accordingly in that A row.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.750
What you wanna see is that blue diagonal

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.950
where it would be 100% of the actual genus

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:52.790
was predicted to be A at the bottom.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.930
But sometimes there is some confusion.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:58.900
It's true that the model
is certainly not perfect.

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.320
And oftentimes, that confusion happens

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:03.680
between things that are closely related.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.410
So in this case, we're
looking at three genus

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.250
from a family of what's
called, the tree ferns.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.730
And there tended to be more confusion say,

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:16.370
within this family than outside of it.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.230
But the model was
incredibly accurate overall.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:24.230
At 97% accuracy to identify the
genus in our 86 genus model.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:26.836
And that accuracy didn't change too much

00:11:26.836 --> 00:11:30.350
when we moved to the 269 genus model.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:32.080
And you can see that
it was highly accurate

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:34.510
across that diagonal, but it's those boxes

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:36.830
that contain families of genera,

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.760
where you see more confusion.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.480
So that's telling us that
the model is operating

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.770
according to the biologist's intuition.

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.150
It's not accidentally making mistakes,

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:50.010
too widely outside of the
exact type of mistakes

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.710
that a less-trained botanist might make

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.580
if they were making
identifications in the field,

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.180
or if they weren't using a hand lens.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.382
Many of these genera are
distinguished by traits

00:11:59.382 --> 00:12:03.550
that are essentially not
visible by the human eye.

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:06.350
So it's pretty incredible in that way,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:09.690
that the machine is
actually learning patterns

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.640
of differences that we don't currently use

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.140
to differentiate between them.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.918
So when we trained our model on species,

00:12:19.918 --> 00:12:23.470
it remained a highly accurate model

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:27.072
and some of the inaccuracies
were, I think, forgivable.

00:12:27.072 --> 00:12:30.450
First off, I just am
showing you two plots.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:33.700
These are kind of basic
metrics that you gather

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:36.880
as you train a neural
network, and we talked

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:39.000
a little bit this morning about EPICs,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:43.230
which are individual
periods where you cycle

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:47.190
your entire training
dataset through the model.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.440
And so the model is constantly changing.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.320
What we're doing is
essentially feeding images

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.200
through this model, it has
some prediction at the end

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.710
and there's an error with that prediction

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:58.810
and we want to modify the parameters

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.580
in a way that would reduce that error.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.820
So that error is the loss that your seeing

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:05.230
here on the Y-axis on this panel.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.348
We want that loss to be plummeting

00:13:07.348 --> 00:13:09.740
as we're training the model.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:13.290
And there's a loss both based
on the training dataset,

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.200
and on the validation training set,

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:18.470
or on the validation set, and
usually what you end up with

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.720
is that the training set has a lower loss

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:23.590
because it's starting to
converge on specific things

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:25.160
that it's learning about those images.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:30.160
So as soon as that starts to
plateau, you stop training.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.030
But we also have accuracy, the accuracy is

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:36.340
both the exact match accuracy,

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:39.370
but also the accuracy in the top five.

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:42.280
So in this case, I'm showing
the exact match accuracy,

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.600
which is about 81.5% for species.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.190
But for the top five predictions,
it gets the right species

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:52.780
in that top five at 95.6% of the time.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:55.040
And that is pretty incredible.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:57.885
Essentially, a lot of species
are just differentiated

00:13:57.885 --> 00:14:02.168
by genetic markers, so the
actual shape differences

00:14:02.168 --> 00:14:06.070
that the images are going
to be able pick up on

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:08.290
are almost nonexistent, and so,

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.490
for the model to get
that level of accuracy

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:13.123
I think is a forgivable level of error.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:19.090
So what is this FernNet using
to make it's identifications?

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.037
Here I'm showing you four example images

00:14:21.037 --> 00:14:23.790
that were passed through the network.

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:27.220
And then four activation
maps, essentially,

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:30.440
these are the pixels that the neural net

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:34.300
found most interesting and most relevant

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:37.780
to making a prediction.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.180
And the good thing is, is that the heat

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:43.313
or the hot colors in these heat maps

00:14:43.313 --> 00:14:48.191
are over top of the plant,
and not on the label.

00:14:48.191 --> 00:14:49.110
(laughing)

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:51.020
It's not learning how to read the label,

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.290
it's learning something about the images.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.710
We can also make some really

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:58.210
cool things called feature maps.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.210
Essentially, what I'm gonna show you next

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:05.210
is the different output of specific layers

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:07.530
in our neural network and essentially,

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.580
what we would be looking at is

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.210
what the filter might look like that would

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.290
activate for that layer of the model.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:18.470
Earlier layers in these
image recognition models

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:23.470
tend to look more like shapes and patterns

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:27.980
and high level layers
tend to be very specific

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:30.440
for making those final distinctions

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:32.683
between say, species A or species B.

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:37.400
So here are four example feature maps.

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:39.560
There are hundreds of
layers in this model,

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:42.300
so I can't show you all
of the feature maps,

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:46.390
but how they've built these
is by randomly populating

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:50.400
an image with colors, and
then optimizing that image

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:53.290
so that it would maximally
activate that layer.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:56.347
So yes, they look
technicolored and more like

00:15:56.347 --> 00:15:59.332
the 90s color palette of neon,

00:15:59.332 --> 00:16:02.990
but the colors don't matter very much.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.736
And that's, I think, a
really important part about

00:16:05.736 --> 00:16:08.690
our model, is that it's
not activating by color,

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.290
the colors tend to be very invariant.

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:12.930
The shapes are highly variable

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.270
and it's really more about shape.

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:17.340
So you can see that later
filter, maybe if you squint

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:22.030
your eyes, it looks a little
bit like a complex fern frond.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:26.050
So it's kind of getting
towards our fern shapes.

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:30.700
Okay, we saw a little bit about why or how

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:34.057
these models work to generate

00:16:34.057 --> 00:16:38.180
from a very complex data input,

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:41.793
a very low dimensional or single,
ah, two dimensional vector

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.490
at the very end, and that vector,

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:50.843
the 1 x 496 vector, right before
it gets to 1 x 1425 vector,

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:55.150
which is actually the
probability that our image

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:59.360
is within the 1425 different levels,

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.140
that 496 vector is the
one that we wanna project

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.150
in our UMAP space like
we saw earlier today

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:07.410
with the PixPlot projections.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.890
In this case, we're using a model

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.370
that was specifically
trained to understand

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:15.660
the differences of
specie shapes for ferns.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:20.660
So we're not using the larger,
kind of front-end model

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:25.440
from ImageNet or ResNet
or something like that

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:27.520
that's pre-trained, we're
using a model that's trained

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:29.490
on these things so we think
those feature embeddings

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:33.850
at 496 vector is actually
a really, a useful vector

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:36.970
not for trying to understand the variation

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:39.230
in say, the label or this color ramp,

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:40.880
because every one of
these images has that.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:42.028
So the model should have basically

00:17:42.028 --> 00:17:45.190
stopped learning about those.

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:47.340
So we take those and we project them

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.650
into a two dimensional space

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:54.310
and we can get our shape space for ferns.

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:56.030
So in this case, what we're looking at

00:17:56.030 --> 00:18:00.410
is a two dimensional
projection of each point

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:03.080
is representative of one of those images.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.332
Just like we saw earlier
today with the cool

00:18:05.332 --> 00:18:08.690
visualizations that PixPlot generated.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:12.160
We could look at this
image and kind of drill in

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:15.490
and look at each individual specimen

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:20.220
and where it's positioned
within this shape space.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:24.897
So here I've just thrown
up two example images

00:18:24.897 --> 00:18:27.280
and we see that they look quite different

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:30.286
from one another, there is
actually a Euclidean distance

00:18:30.286 --> 00:18:32.740
that is preserved in this projection,

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:35.170
so we can measure the
distance between them

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:40.170
as some aspect of their shape
dissimilarity or similarity.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.430
And we can then use that to understand

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.690
this latitudinal diversity gradient.

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:48.150
So, here I'm actually now
just dividing the world,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:51.070
instead of looking at latitudinal
differences continuously,

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:56.070
I've just divided the world
into four unique groupings.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:19:00.130
Tropical climates,
mountain desert climates,

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:02.440
temperate climates for the blue ones,

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.080
and the dark blue is the polar climates.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:07.370
And now I'm just gonna
label our shape space

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:08.823
based on whether or not our ferns

00:19:08.823 --> 00:19:11.560
fell into these categories.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:14.800
And lo and behold, there's
definitely some clustering

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:19.550
related to where those ferns are evolving,

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:21.840
where they're operating ecologically

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.880
and where they are in
our shape dimensions.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:26.830
You can see that there's kind of a cluster

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:29.045
of temperate things, it
kinda surrounds this,

00:19:29.045 --> 00:19:33.350
maybe other cluster of tropical
things, which is very cool,

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:34.830
and then there's this all this scatter

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:38.230
and a lot of that scatter
is tropical plants.

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:41.992
And so that tells us that
not only is this operating

00:19:41.992 --> 00:19:45.330
this species' richness,
this number of species

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.020
in a given location is
operating on the creating

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:50.740
more genetic diversity,
but it also potentially,

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:53.420
is creating or associated with more

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:56.000
shape diversity or ecological diversity.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:57.930
So how does morphological shape

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.990
states evolve according to latitude?

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:01.440
Or vary according to latitude?

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.650
I think this is kind of the first step

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:07.020
at really accessing this,
one of these projections,

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:10.770
we could kind of create a
way to redo this process

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:13.060
and look more deeply at it statistically,

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:16.700
but essentially, there are
some kind of unique variations.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:20.270
And the next step is
to kind of combine that

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:22.730
with our analysis of the species' numbers.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.010
And so that's what we're working on now.

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:27.690
So with that, many contributors to this.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.330
We've heard about this
botany digitization project,

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:31.743
I think a number of times.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:36.840
Huge thanks to the efforts
that are not only going

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:39.050
into digitizing the botany collection,

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:41.877
that's happening at Peabody and many other

00:20:41.877 --> 00:20:43.937
Natural History museums around the world,

00:20:43.937 --> 00:20:46.170
but the efforts to
digitize these collections

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:49.960
are really the only
way that we can perform

00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:51.980
analysis of this scale,
so thanks very much.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:55.147
(audience applauding)

00:20:59.772 --> 00:21:02.355
(gentle music)

